# What is NOT sent to the Docker daemon when deploy/Dockerfile is built.
#
# This file must live HERE, at the repository root, because the build context is
# the repository root (deploy/docker-compose.yml → build.context: ..). Docker
# reads only `<context>/.dockerignore`; the copy that used to sit at
# deploy/.dockerignore was never read by anything, so none of its rules — the
# `**/.env` one included — had ever taken effect.
#
# Matching is Docker's, not the shell's, and the difference bites:
#   * a single `*` does NOT cross a `/`, so `*.md` hides the repo-root
#     documents and nothing deeper. That is deliberate below: the wiki IS a
#     tree of .md files and the image has to carry it, so there is no `**/*.md`
#     rule anywhere in here.
#   * `**/x` matches `x` at any depth, including the root.
#   * a later `!` line re-includes what an earlier line excluded.
#
# The image needs exactly two things from this context: app/backend/ (minus its
# venv, caches and tests) and virtualagent/resources/. Everything listed here is
# either not one of those, or is a copy of a developer's machine.

# Git and CI metadata
.git
.gitattributes
.gitignore
.github

# Editors and agent scratch
.vscode
.idea
.claude

# Secrets. Never in an image layer, at any depth. The Dockerfile copies nothing
# from app/ except app/backend/, but app/.env is one mistyped COPY away and the
# rule costs nothing.
**/.env
**/.env.*

# Python environments and caches. `**/.venv` is the important one: a developer
# or a deploy host that has run `uv sync` in app/backend has a whole OS-specific
# interpreter tree there (~111M), and it used to be copied over the Linux venv
# the image builds. The Dockerfile now also builds its environment at /opt/venv,
# outside the copied tree, so this is the first of two defences, not the only one.
**/.venv
**/venv
**/__pycache__
**/*.pyc
**/*.pyo
**/.pytest_cache
**/.mypy_cache
**/.ruff_cache
**/.coverage
**/coverage
**/htmlcov

# The service's own tests and their fixture wiki. They are the gate's business,
# not production's, and the fixture wiki must never be mistaken for knowledge.
app/backend/tests

# Nothing else in the repository belongs in the image: the iOS app is built on a
# Mac, the harness is the gate, tools/ has its own uv projects, and the factory
# scaffolding is not runtime.
app/ios
harness
tools
scripts
docs
.archon
.factory

# The deploy files describe the stack from outside the container. The Dockerfile
# itself is re-included: BuildKit reads it through a separate path, but the
# legacy builder reads it from the context, and a build that cannot find its own
# Dockerfile is a confusing way to learn that.
deploy/*
!deploy/Dockerfile

# Repo-root governance and documentation. Root level only, by design — see the
# note about `*` and `/` at the top: the wiki's .md files must survive this file.
*.md
